    {
      "title": "Adolf Hitler",
      "source": "Textbook",
      "note": "",
      "text": "After World War I, Germany was in chaos. It's new government, called the **Weimar Republic** had signed the Treaty of Versailles. The treaty had blamed the war on Germany, redistributed German land, banned the German military and forced Germany to pay massive amounts of **reparations** (payments made for war). When the Great Depression hit, Germans demanded a government that could provide jobs and rebuild German pride. The man who sought to deliver that government was Adolf Hitler. Hitler had watched Mussolini's rise to power and wanted to imitate his actions. Hitler restored German pride by proclaiming that Germans belonged to a superior, “Aryan” race. He blamed much of Germany's problems on one percent of the German population, the Jews.\n\nHitler won power in Germany's democratic government in 1933 through his political party the, National Socialist German Workers or Nazi, party. Like Mussolini, Hitler was a dictator. Once in power, he banned all other political parties and had his opponents arrested or killed. Hitler's elite, black uniformed unit called the **SS** arrested and murdered hundreds of Hitler's enemies. Their brutal actions and the terror created by the **Gestapo**, the Nazi secret police, scared most Germans into total obedience of Hitler. The Nazis also passed laws depriving Jews of most of their rights. Simultaneously, Hitler began his mission of finding more **lebensraum**, or German living space. Hitler maintained that Germany was overcrowded and needed more land to accommodate his superior race. Most Germans accepted the loss of freedoms. They did not question Nazi racism. They let Hitler blame troubles on the Jews. They even welcomed his military buildup, an act outlawed by the Versailles treaty.",
      "set": "Fascist Leaders",
      "_order": 2,
      "questions": [],
      "image": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zewolff1/us-history-data/main/images/5-1/hitler.png"
    },
    {
      "title": "Joseph Stalin",
      "source": "Textbook",
      "note": "",
      "text": "Joseph Stalin was a part of the Bolshevik Revolution that transformed Russia into the Soviet Union. He took over as dictator after the death of Vladimir Lenin. Stalin destroyed all those he believed were against him. Stalin had his main opponent, Leon Trotsky exiled from the Soviet Union and eventually assassinated. Stalin turned the Soviet Union into a **totalitarian state.** In this form of government, a dictatorship controls and regulates every aspect of the lives of its citizens. Stalin's police arrested millions and had them shot or sent to labor camps. He brought all factories and farms under government control. Stalin used propaganda to make himself a hero. His pictures appeared everywhere. Stalin also **censored,** or withheld, news from inside and outside the Soviet Union. Stalin used his power to move the Soviets to a strong industrialized economy. His government made all economic decisions to increase industry and farming. They set production goals, forcing Russians to work day and night. Workers who did not meet production goals were punished. While production levels went up, most Russians were poor. Their standard of living and spirits were low.",
